window.__registerChapter({"num": 58, "slug": "058_Type_I_Interferonopathies", "title": "Type I Interferonopathies", "authors": "Christopher J.A. Duncan, Tracy Briggs", "part_num": "X", "part_title": "Immune/Inflammatory Disorders", "word_count": 6522, "pearls": ["Autoimmune manifestations and autoantibodies may also be noted; some patients fulfil diagnostic criteria for specific autoimmune disorders such as SLE.", "Autosomal recessive ISG15 deficiency is linked to hypomorphic USP18 function through enhanced USP18 protein degradation, producing a milder form of inflammatory disease.", "Many of the variants in causative genes are recessively inherited, but autosomal dominant forms are also seen and so a three-generation pedigree is a valuable part of the clinical history (Table 58.1).", "SMS is caused by autosomal dominant variants in either IFH1 or RIGI (formerly DDX58 ), encoding RNA sensors MDA5 and RIG-I, respectively.", "Secondary to autosomal recessive variants in the acid phosphatase 5, tartrate resistant (ACP5) gene, this condition was originally thought to be a skeletal dysplasia, with enchondromas and platyspondyly, causing progressive short stature.", "Children with typical features of AGS or SPENCD may fulfill the diagnostic criteria for SLE; heterozygous variants in TREX1, IFIH1, or ACP5 may be associated with adult onset SLE, without the other associated features.", "Disorders of negative feedback upon IFNAR are associated with autosomal recessive variants in USP18 and STAT2, exhibiting features of severe, early-onset AGS—also described as pseudo-TORCH syndrome .", "Patients with autosomal recessive ISG15 deficiency are generally less severely affected, experiencing seizures that are associated with asymptomatic intracranial calcification, consistent with a hypomorphic defect of USP18 protein; necrotizing cutaneous inflammatory disease has also been reported."], "genes": ["ACP5", "ADAR1", "AIRE", "ATAD3A", "C1QA", "C1QB", "C1QC", "C2", "C3", "DDX58", "DNASE1", "DNASE1L3", "DNASE2", "IFH1", "IFIH1", "ISG15", "JAK1", "LSM11"], "omims": [], "subtopics": ["Clinical Recognition", "Molecular Mechanism", "Interferon Induction and Signaling", "Defective Nucleic Acid Homeostasis", "Constitutive Pattern Recognition Receptor (PRR) Signaling", "Exaggerated IFNAR Signaling", "Individual Clinical Syndromes/ Molecular Defects", "Aicardi-Goutières Syndrome (AGS)", "Familial Chilblain Lupus (FCL)", "Singleton-Merten Syndrome (SMS)", "Spondyloenchondrodysplasia (SPENCD)", "Monogenic Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)", "STING-Associated Vasculopathy of Infancy (SAVI) and COPA Syndrome", "USP18 and ISG15 Deficiency, STAT2 - Associated Type I Interferonopathy"], "figs": [{"img": "fig_p1305_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 58.1", "caption": "Schematic representation of genetic mechanisms and clinical manifestations in main interferonopathies."}, {"img": "fig_p1308_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 58.2", "caption": "Characteristic clinical phenotypes of interferonopathies. Chilblain lesions typical of monogenic type I interferonopathies, seen most frequently on the toes, fingers (A), ears (B), and nose. The lesions are generally worse in the cold months. (C) Representative brain CT scan of intracranial calcifications seen in a patient with Aicardi-Goutières syndrome due to TREX1 homozygous pathogenic variants. (D) Chest CT scan of a SAVI patient (performed at the age of 12.5 years) showing evidence of interstitial lung disease (interlobular septal thickening, intralobular lines, cystic lesions, and some ground-glass lesions) and fibrosis (honeycombing). (Modified from Melki I, Fremond ML. Type I interferonopathies: from a novel concept to targeted therapeutics. Curr Rheum Reports . 2020;22:32, Fig. 1.)"}, {"img": "fig_p1309_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 58.3", "caption": "Schematic representation of various pathways affected in genetic interferonopathies. Purple rectangles indicate variant proteins in type I interferonopathies. Numbered yellow circles indicate the common mechanistic defect. 1. Loss-of-function pathogenic variants of genes that encode enzymes responsible for DNA or DNA-RNA hybrid molecule degradation. 2. Pathogenic variants that lead to constitutive activation or reduction of activation threshold of intracytosolic nucleic acid sensors. 3. Gain-of-function pathogenic variants of positive IFN signaling regulators; 4. Loss-offunction pathogenic variants of negative IFN signaling regulators; and 5. Proteasomal dysfunctional leading to the unfolded protein response and downstream IFN pathway activation. ADAR, RNA adenosine deaminase; ERGIC, endothelial reticulum–Golgi intermediate compartment; IFIH1, IFN-induced helicase C domain-containing protein 1; IFNAR, interferon-α receptor; IRF3, interferon regulatory factor 3; IRF7, interferon regulatory factor 7; IRF9, interferon regulatory factor 9; ISG15, interferon-stimulated gene 15; ISGF3, the transcriptional activator induced by interferon-α; ISRE, interferon-sensitive response element; JAK1, Janus kinase 1; MAVS, mitochondrial antiviral-signaling protein; NTPs, nucleoside triphosphates; P, phosphorylation; RIG-I, retinoic acid-inducible gene I; RNASEH2, ribonuclease H domain 2; SAMHD1, SAM domain and HD domain deoxynucl"}, {"img": "fig_p1312_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 58.4", "caption": "Clinical manifestations of stimulator of interferon genes (STING)–associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy (SAVI). (A) Typical facial distribution of telangiectatic lesions on the nose and cheeks with atrophy and scarring of the skin with loss of deep tissue of the nose. (B) Violaceous, scaling, atrophic plaques on both hands and progressive autoamputations of several fingers. (C) Hilar lymphadenopathy and bilateral interstitial infiltrates on high-resolution computed tomography (CT) image. (D) Ulcerated lesions on the pinna of the ear with scales and crusts."}], "tables_meta": [{"label": "Table 58.1", "title": "Gene Variant Mechanism (GOF/LOF) Inheritance Protein Function Phenotype(s)"}, {"label": "TABLE 58.2", "title": "Suggestive Features of Type I Interferonopathies"}, {"label": "TABLE 58.2", "title": "Suggestive Features of Type I Interferonopathies—cont’d"}, {"label": "Table 58.4", "title": "AGS Screening for glaucoma and hypothyroidism, monitoring of liver function, provision of antiepileptics, enteral nutrit"}], "body_md": "\n<!-- p.1302 -->\n\n# 58 Type I Interferonopathies \n\n**CHRISTOPHER J.A. DUNCAN, TRACY BRIGGS** \n\nThere are approximately 40 putative monogenic type I interferonopathies; this grouping is included in the International Union of Immunological Societies (IUIS) classification of inborn errors of immunity (Table 58.1). \n\n## **Clinical Recognition** \n\nThe type I interferonopathies are a heterogenous group of disorders, with variable, multisystem clinical features, accompanied by shared biomarker evidence of elevated IFNI activity. Whilst clinical manifestations vary by genotype (Fig. 58.1 and Table 58.2), certain commonalities are recognized, for example intracranial calcification is observed in ~50% of genotypes and vasculitic skin manifestations also frequently overlap. Typically early onset and of varying severity, these disorders cause significant morbidity and mortality. In keeping with their sterile inflammatory nature, fever and other signs of infection may be observed, without an identified pathogen. Autoimmune manifestations and autoantibodies may also be noted; some patients fulfil diagnostic criteria for specific autoimmune disorders such as SLE. Most interferonopathies exhibit a neurological phenotype, ranging from isolated spastic paraparesis to severe neurological disability, accompanied by neuroimaging abnormalities including leukodystrophy, calcification and microcephaly (Fig. 58.2). Musculoskeletal, ocular, cardiac, or pulmonary disease variably manifest, emphasizing the multisystem nature of these disorders. The spectrum of clinical features can delay diagnosis. Sometimes the diagnosis may not be considered until a second child is born into the family with similar manifestations, raising the possibility of a genetic disorder. A genetic diagnosis may be made more challenging secondary to variable expression of the disease manifestations for a given genotype, even within the same family. Individuals bearing pathogenic variants with relatively mild adult-onset disease or no clinical manifestations at all **(clinical nonpenetrance)** , which can complicate aspects of clinical management, genetic counselling, and assessment of outcome in clinical trials. \n\n## **Molecular Mechanism** \n\nConsistent with the association of these disorders with biomarkers of IFN-I overactivity, most genes associated with type I interferonopathies encode proteins that function in some way in either (1) the induction or regulation of IFN-I signaling or (2) in cellular processes involved in nucleic acid homeostasis leading to the accumulation and/or inappropriate subcellular localization of host-derived molecules that inadvertently trigger the IFN-I pathway (Fig. 58.3). These disorders have informed our fundamental understanding of innate immunity, providing clinical evidence that host-derived nucleic acids are capable of activating pathways that primarily evolved to detect pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs). These disorders serve to indicate the pathological consequences of dysregulated IFN-I activity, they also provide evidence that IFN-I independent mechanisms of disease also contribute, either via the induction of parallel programs of innate immune signaling, and/or from direct impacts to cellular homeostasis attributable to the molecular defect itself. In this context, examples of _purer_ IFN-I mediated diseases may arise from dysfunction of negative feedback upon the receptor for IFN-I. The molecular association of several genotypes with heightened IFN-I activity remains obscure (i.e., the group of defects of proteasomal homeostasis). \n\n### **Interferon Induction and Signaling** \n\nA variety of molecular pattern recognition receptors (PRRs) detect the presence of invading microbes via recognition of PAMPs, which in the context of interferonopathies are typically species of nucleic acid. The challenge of distinguishing these from host-derived nucleic acids is aided by biochemical properties of pathogen encoded nucleic acid molecules and/or by their localization to cellular compartments such as the cytosol, where they are not typically encountered in normal cellular homeostasis. Under certain conditions these pathways can be triggered by host-derived nucleic acids. Cells express multiple PRRs capable of\n\n<!-- p.1303 -->\n\n|**TABLE**<br>**58.1**|\n|---|\n\n\n#### **Type I Interferonopathies** \n\n|Gene|Variant<br>Mechanism<br>(GOF/LOF)|Inheritance|Protein Function|Phenotype(s)|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|_TREX1_|LOF|AR, AD (DN)|DNase|AGS, FCL, SLE|\n|_RNASEH2A_|LOF|AR|RNase|AGS|\n|_RNASEH2B_|LOF|AR|RNase|AGS, SP|\n|_RNASEH2C_|LOF|AR|RNase|AGS|\n|_SAMHD1_|LOF|AR|Regulation of dNTP|AGS, FCL, cerebral vasculopathy|\n|_ADAR1_|LOF|AR, AD (DN)|RNA editing|AGS, BSN, SP|\n|_IFIH1_|GOF|AD|dsRNA sensor|AGS, SMS, SP|\n|_LSM11_|LOF|AR|RDH pre-mRNA processing|AGS|\n|_RNU7-1_|LOF|AR|RDH pre-mRNA processing|AGS|\n|_DDX58_|GOF|AD|dsRNA sensor|SMS|\n|_DNASE2_|LOF|AR|DNase|Neonatal anemia, arthropathy, glomerulonephritis,<br>and liver disease|\n|_POLA1_|LOF|XLR|DNA polymerase|X-linked reticulate pigmentary disorder|\n|_ATAD3A_|LOF|AD (DN)|Multiple|NDD, systemic sclerosis, SP|\n|_PNPT1_|LOF|AR|Polynucleotide phosphorylase|Infantile encephalopathy (mitochondrial disease), BSN|\n|_ACP5_|LOF|AR|Phosphatase|SPENCD, SLE|\n|_C1QA_|LOF|AR|Alternative complement pathway|SLE|\n|_C1QB_|LOF|AR|Alternative complement pathway|SLE|\n|_C1QC_|LOF|AR|Alternative complement pathway|SLE|\n|_TLR7_|GOF|XLD|Toll like receptor signalling|SLE|\n|_STING1_|GOF|AD, AR|Adaptor, DNA sensing|STING-associated vasculopathy with onset in<br>infancy, FCL|\n|_COPA_|LOF|AD (DN)|ER to Golgi transport|ILD, pulmonary hemorrhage, arthropathy,<br>glomerulonephritis,|\n|_USP18_|LOF|AR|IFNAR regulation|PTORCH, AGS-like|\n|_STAT2_|LOF|AR|IFNAR regulation|PTORCH, AGS-like|\n|_ISG15_|LOF|AR|USP18 stability|ICC, MSMD|\n|_SOCS1_|LOF|AD|JAK-STAT regulation|Autoimmunity (including SLE), immune<br>dysregulation, immunodeficiency|\n|_JAK1_|GOF|AD|IFN signaling|Eosinophilic inflammatory disease, immune<br>dysregulation|\n|_STAT1_|GOF|AD|IFN signaling|CMC, Autoimmunity, ICC|\n|_PSMB8_|LOF|AR|Proteosome|PRAAS|\n|_PSMB4_|LOF|AR|Proteosome|PRAAS|\n|_PSMA3_|LOF|AR|Proteosome|PRAAS|\n|_PSMB9_|LOF|AR|Proteosome|PRAAS|\n|_POMP_|LOF|AD (DN)|Proteosome|PRAAS|\n|_PSMB10_|LOF|AR|Proteosome|PRAAS|\n|_PSMG2_|LOF|AR|Proteosome|PRAAS|\n|_PSMB12_|LOF|AD|Proteosome|NDD|\n\n\n_AD,_ Autosomal dominant; _AGS,_ Aicardi-Goutières syndrome; _AR,_ autosomal recessive; _BSN,_ bilateral striatal necrosis; _CMC,_ chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis; _ER,_ endoplasmic reticulum; _FCL,_ familial chilblain lupus; _GOF,_ gain of function; _ICC,_ intracerebral calcification; _ILD,_ interstitial lung disease; _JAK,_ Janus kinase; _LOF,_ loss of function; _NDD,_ neurodevelopmental disease; _PRAAS,_ proteosome-associated autoinflammatory syndromes; _PTORCH,_ pseudo-TORCH syndrome; _RDH,_ replication-dependent histone; _SMS,_ Singleton-Merten syndrome; _SP,_ spastic paraplegia; _SPENCD,_ spondyloenchondrodysplasia; _STAT,_ signal transducer and activator of transcription; _XLD,_ X-linked dominant; _XLR,_ X-linked recessive.\n\n<!-- p.1304 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p1305_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 58.1** Schematic representation of genetic mechanisms and clinical manifestations in main interferonopathies. (From d’Angelo DM, Di Filippo P, Brenda L, Chiarelli F: Type I interferonopathies in children: an overview. _Front Pediatr_ . 2021;9:Article 631329 [Fig. 2].) \n\ndetecting a range of PAMPs. These include Toll-like receptors (TLRs), expressed either at the cell surface (TLR1-5) or within endosomes (TLR3/7/8/9), alongside cytosolic sensors of RNA (the retinoic acid inducible gene [RIG]-like receptors, such as RIG-I and melanoma differentiation-associated protein 5 [MDA5]) or DNA (e.g., cyclic GMP-AMP synthase [cGAS] and interferon gamma inducible protein 16 [IFI16]). Activation of PRRs triggers, via distinct adaptor \n\nproteins (e.g., mitochondrial antiviral signaling protein [MAVS] for RIG-I/MDA5 and stimulator of interferon genes [STING] for cGAS/IFI16), a molecular cascade of events culminating in phosphorylation of the kinases TANKbinding kinase 1 (TBK1) and I-kappa-B kinase epsilon (IKKε) and subsequent nuclear translocation of interferon regulatory factors 3 and 7 (IRF3 and IRF7), alongside activation of other pathways such as nuclear factor kappa B\n\n<!-- p.1305 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 58.2** Suggestive Features of Type I Interferonopathies\n\n|Familial history|Several affected individuals, even if phenotypic spectrum might be variable within the same family|\n|---|---|\n|Age of onset|• Young age at onset, in favor of monogenic disease<br>• Later onset also reported|\n|Neurological phenotypes|Clinical signs:|\n||• Spasticity, spastic paraparesis|\n||• Acute or subacute dystonia|\n||• Encephalopathy with seizures and progressive microcephaly|\n||• Cortical blindness|\n||• Variable developmental delay|\n||• Ataxia|\n||• Psychosis|\n||• Vascular neurological disease (Moya Moya)/strokes|\n||• Rare demyelinating or multifocal neuropathies|\n||Lumbar puncture:|\n||• Meningitis (inconstant)|\n||• Elevated pterins/neopterins in the CSF|\n||• Elevated IFN-αactivity or protein in the CSF|\n||Morphological imaging features:|\n||• CT scans: ICC, basal ganglia calcifications or progressive cerebral atrophy|\n||• Cerebral MRI: leukoencephalopathy, white matter rarefaction, delayed myelination, bilateral<br>striatal necrosis, deep white matter cysts, or intracranial aneurysms|\n|Cutaneous features|• Chilblains/FCL; necrotizing vasculitis of fingers, toes, helix, cheeks, and nose, telangiectasia,<br>cutaneous ulcerations|\n||• Livedo reticularis|\n||• Panniculitis, violaceous periorbital rash|\n||• Lentigines|\n||• Psoriasis|\n||• Nail dystrophy, sparse hair|\n|Failure to thrive/short<br>stature|• Secondary to osseous dysplasia_(ACP5)_|\n||• Secondary to the inflammatory status_(TMEM173)_|\n|Systemic inflammation<br>and immune features|• Recurrent fevers|\n||• Autoimmune features/SLE/autoantibodies (not necessarily specific)|\n||• Inconstant immune deficiency|\n||• Elevated IFN I pathway in whole blood/serum/plasma|\n|Hematological|• AI anemia, dyserythropoiesis|\n||• Thrombocytopenia or thrombocytosis|\n||• Malignancies (chronic lymphocytic leukemia, cutaneous T cell lymphoma,_SAMHD1_)|\n|Lung features|• Interstitial lung disease (isolated or not)|\n||• Lung fibrosis|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.1306 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 58.2** Suggestive Features of Type I Interferonopathies—cont’d\n\n||• Intraalveolar hemorrhage|\n|---|---|\n||• Macrophagic alveolitis<br>i|\n|Vascular features<br>|• Calcification of the aorta or blood vessels<br>|\n|Musculoskeletal features|• Joint pain, arthritis|\n||• Contractures and joint retractions|\n||• Jaccoud arthropathy, tendon rupture|\n||• Muscle weakness and pain/myositis<br>• Calcinosis<br>• X-rays: acro-osteolysis, wide medullar cavities phalange, deforming arthropathies/joint subluxation<br>with conserved interarticular space, calcinosis<br>|\n|Ophthalmological features|• Glaucoma|\n||• Papillary edema*|\n|Kidney features|• Lupus nephritis|\n|Gastrointestinal features|• VEO-IBD (very severe protein-losing enteropathy)|\n|Dental anomalies|• Retained primary teeth<sup>†</sup>|\n|*Reported in one SAVI patient tre<br>_†_Data not published.<br>_Ab,_Autoantibodies;_AI,_autoimm<br>interferon;_IFN I,_type I interferon;<br>From Melki I, Fremond ML. Type|• Early loss of permanent teeth<br>ated with JAK1/2 inhibitor.<br>une;_CSF,_cerebrospinal fluid;_CT,_computerized tomography;_FCL,_familial chilblain lupus;_ICC,_intracranial calcifications;_IFN,_<br>_MRI,_magnetic resonance imaging;_SLE,_systemic lupus erythematosus;_VEO-IBD,_very-early-onset inflammatory bowel disease.<br>I interferonopathies: From a novel concept to targeted therapeutics._Curr Rheum Reports_. 2020;22:3, Table 2.|\n\n\n(NF-κB). These transcription factors in turn induce the expression of _IFN_ and proinflammatory cytokine genes, respectively. \n\nThe type I IFNs comprise 12 subtypes of IFNα, alongside IFNβ, IFNω, IFNκ, and IFNε, and all signal through the heterodimeric IFNα/β receptor (IFNAR), composed of IFNAR1 and IFNAR2 subunits. IFNAR signaling activates an intracellular Janus kinase (JAK)–signal transducer and activator of transcription (STAT) signaling cascade culminating in the assembly of transcription factor complexes that regulate the expression of interferon-stimulated genes (ISGs). This primarily involves the receptor-associated kinases JAK1 and tyrosine kinase 2 (TYK2), which activate a heterotrimeric complex of STAT1, STAT2, and IRF9, known as interferon stimulated gene factor 3 (ISGF3), via phosphorylation of tyrosine residues on STAT1 and STAT2. A minor complex comprising tyrosine phosphorylated STAT1 homodimers also participates in IFNAR signaling. These transcription factor complexes bind interferon stimulated response elements or gamma activated sequence elements respectively in ISG promoters. This process is subject to tight negative regulation, primarily mediated by ubiquitin specific protease 18 (USP18), an ISG which displaces JAK1 from IFNAR2 in a STAT2-dependent manner. Other more broadly active negative regulators of JAK-STAT1 signaling, such as suppressor of cytokine signaling 1 (SOCS1), also participate. \n\nIt should also be noted that additional IFN subtypes exist, all of which activate intracellular JAK-STAT signaling cascades shared with IFN-I, culminating in the induction of partially overlapping ISG programs. A single type II interferon, IFNγ, is more typically associated with lymphocyte effector function in the context of adaptive immunity and acts through the IFNγ receptor and tyrosine phosphorylated STAT1 homodimers also trigger the assembly of ISGF3. Similarly, four subtypes of type III interferon (IFNλ1-4) which like IFN-I also primarily trigger assembly of ISGF3. Unlike IFNAR, the receptor for IFN-III is considered more restricted in its expression, confined mainly to epithelial cells and some immune cell types, and generally absent from the central nervous system (CNS), a major locus of pathology in the interferonopathies. The potential contribution of these IFN subtypes to interferonopathy pathogenesis remains uncertain. \n\n### **Defective Nucleic Acid Homeostasis** \n\nA central insight from the monogenic interferonopathies is the capacity of host nucleic acid species to trigger innate immunity. Indeed, most variants associated with **AicardiGoutières syndrome (AGS)** confer defects in nucleic acid homeostasis (an exception is variants of the interferon induced with helicase C domain 1 _[IFIH1]_ gene, encoding the RNA sensor MDA5). Specific examples of genes\n\n<!-- p.1307 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p1308_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 58.2** Characteristic clinical phenotypes of interferonopathies. Chilblain lesions typical of monogenic type I interferonopathies, seen most frequently on the toes, fingers (A), ears (B), and nose. The lesions are generally worse in the cold months. (C) Representative brain CT scan of intracranial calcifications seen in a patient with Aicardi-Goutières syndrome due to _TREX1_ homozygous pathogenic variants. (D) Chest CT scan of a SAVI patient (performed at the age of 12.5 years) showing evidence of interstitial lung disease (interlobular septal thickening, intralobular lines, cystic lesions, and some ground-glass lesions) and fibrosis (honeycombing).  (Modified from Melki I, Fremond ML. Type I interferonopathies: from a novel concept to targeted therapeutics. _Curr Rheum Reports_ . 2020;22:32, Fig. 1.) \n\ninvolved in the type I interferonopathies with roles in nucleic acid homeostasis include those encoding three prime repair exonuclease 1 (TREX1) and lysosomal deoxyribonuclease 2 (DNASE2), enzymes responsible for degradation of host single stranded and double stranded DNA; SAM domain and HD domain-containing protein 1 (SAMHD1), which controls the hydrolyzation of deoxynucleoside triphosphates (dNTPs); and the heterotrimeric type II ribonuclease H enzyme (RNaseH2) complex encoded by _RNASEH2A_ , _RNASEH2B_ and _RNASEH2C_ , which regulates cellular levels of RNA-DNA hybrids (alongside DNA polymerase-α, encoded by _POLA1_ ). Disruption of other critical homeostatic processes can also trigger innate immunity, such as: (i) histone stoichiometry, disrupted in AGS by variants of the LSM11, U7 small nuclear RNA-associated protein _(LSM11)_ gene and the RNA, U7 small nuclear 1 _(RNU7-1)_ gene, leading to inappropriate sensing of nuclear \n\nDNA; or (ii) mitochondrial integrity, perturbed by variant of the ATPase family AAA domain-containing protein 3A _(ATAD3A)_ gene, thought to lead to cytosolic leakage of mitochondrial DNA. Collectively, these defects are associated with induction of IFNs and other proinflammatory cytokines via engagement of cGAS-STING signaling. By contrast, defects in the mitochondrial polyribonucleotide nucleotidyltransferase 1 gene lead to activation of cytosolic RIG-I like receptors via accumulation of mitochondrial dsRNA in the cytosol, in a similar process to that encountered in _ATAD3A_ associated disease. \n\n### **Constitutive Pattern Recognition Receptor (PRR) Signaling** \n\nAnother major mechanism underlying the interferonopathies is activating variants in PRRs or their adaptor proteins.\n\n<!-- p.1308 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p1309_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 58.3** Schematic representation of various pathways affected in genetic interferonopathies. _Purple rectangles_ indicate variant proteins in type I interferonopathies. Numbered _yellow circles_ indicate the common mechanistic defect. _1._ Loss-of-function pathogenic variants of genes that encode enzymes responsible for DNA or DNA-RNA hybrid molecule degradation. _2._ Pathogenic variants that lead to constitutive activation or reduction of activation threshold of intracytosolic nucleic acid sensors. _3._ Gain-of-function pathogenic variants of positive IFN signaling regulators; _4._ Loss-offunction pathogenic variants of negative IFN signaling regulators; and _5._ Proteasomal dysfunctional leading to the unfolded protein response and downstream IFN pathway activation. _ADAR,_ RNA adenosine deaminase; _ERGIC,_ endothelial reticulum–Golgi intermediate compartment; _IFIH1,_ IFN-induced helicase C domain-containing protein 1; _IFNAR,_ interferon-α receptor; _IRF3,_ interferon regulatory factor 3; _IRF7,_ interferon regulatory factor 7; _IRF9,_ interferon regulatory factor 9; _ISG15,_ interferon-stimulated gene 15; _ISGF3,_ the transcriptional activator induced by interferon-α; _ISRE,_ interferon-sensitive response element; _JAK1,_ Janus kinase 1; _MAVS,_ mitochondrial antiviral-signaling protein; _NTPs,_ nucleoside triphosphates; _P,_ phosphorylation; _RIG-I,_ retinoic acid-inducible gene I; _RNASEH2,_ ribonuclease H domain 2; _SAMHD1,_ SAM domain and HD domain deoxynucleoside triphosphate triphosphohydrolase 1; _STAT,_ signal transducer and activator of transcription; _STING,_ stimulator of interferon genes; _TBK1,_ TANK-binding kinase 1; _TREX1,_ DNA 3′ repair exonuclease 1; _TYK2,_ tyrosine kinase 2; _USP18,_ ubiquitin-specific peptidase 18. (From Melki I, Fremond ML. Type I interferonopathies: From a novel concept to targeted therapeutics. _Curr Rheum Reports_ . 2020;22:3, Table 2.) \n\nHeterozygous gain-of-function (GOF) variants in the cytosolic RNA sensor MDA5 are thought to lower the threshold of activation by endogenous dsRNA species, triggering autonomous MAVS-dependent TBK1-IRF3 signaling. Similarly, GOF _STING1_ variants lead to constitutive ligand-independent translocation of STING to the Golgi apparatus from the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), resulting in downstream activation of TBK1/IKKε. Also, within this pathway, a dominant negative heterozygous variant of coatomer subunit-α _(COPA)_ prevents retrograde transport of STING back to the ER, leading to a similar immunological/clinical outcome. \n\n### **Exaggerated IFNAR Signaling** \n\nAnother group of molecular defects involve unrestrained signaling downstream of the IFN-I receptor, IFNAR, through either (i) impairment of negative feedback or (ii) gain of intracellular signaling function. The former is mediated by impairment \n\nof an IFNAR regulatory axis that relies primarily on ubiquitin specific protease 18 (USP18), supported by signal transducer and activator of transcription 2 (STAT2) and IFN-stimulated gene 15 (ISG15). USP18 is a key, specific negative regulator of IFNAR signaling, which acts by displacing JAK1 from IFNAR, terminating ongoing signaling. Loss of USP18 expression or function confers a profound defect of negative feedback upon IFNAR, resulting in unusually prolonged signaling in response to IFN-I and a severe, early-onset type I interferonopathy phenotype. This feedback loop also depends on an interaction between USP18 and STAT2, that is in turn compromised by homozygous missense variants in _STAT2_ , producing a recessively inherited disease that in many respects phenocopies _USP18_ deficiency. Autosomal recessive _ISG15_ deficiency is linked to hypomorphic USP18 function through enhanced USP18 protein degradation, producing a milder form of inflammatory disease. \n\nHeterozygous loss of expression and/or function variant of _SOCS1_ , a broad negative regulator of JAK-STAT signaling, is\n\n<!-- p.1309 -->\n\nassociated with a distinct phenotype of early-onset autoimmunity (including SLE), allied to a transcriptional ISG signature and evidence of prolonged ligand-dependent IFNAR signaling at the molecular level. Consistent with the broader regulatory function of SOCS1, this disorder is accompanied by failure to restrain multiple cytokine signaling pathways, including those mediated by interleukin 2 (IL2), IL4 and IFNγ, underpinning a broader defect of immune regulation than that associated with _USP18_ or _STAT2_ lesions. \n\nThe second group of defects involve heterozygous activating variants of genes encoding products acting directly within the IFNAR signaling pathway, such as _JAK1_ and _STAT1_ . Whilst these variants are associated with certain features of type I interferonopathy (for example an elevated ISG signature with cerebral calcification in some patients with STAT1 GOF), GOF variants in _JAK1_ and _STAT1_ have an immunological impact extending beyond the IFN-I system, owing to the participation of JAK1 and STAT1 in multiple cytokine signaling pathways (including IFNγ, IL4, and IL6). In the case of STAT1 GOF variants, excessive STAT1 activity results in attenuation of IL17 responses. As with _SOCS1_ variants discussed above, this accounts for a broader clinical phenotype incorporating features not seen in other type I interferonopathies, such as eosinophilic inflammatory disease in _JAK1_ GOF and chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis in _STAT1_ GOF. \n\n## **Individual Clinical Syndromes/ Molecular Defects** \n\n### **Aicardi-Goutières Syndrome (AGS)** \n\nAGS is caused by variants in nine different genes: _TREX1, RNASEH2A/B/C, SAMHD1,_ double stranded RNA-specific adenosine-deaminase 1 _(ADAR1), IFIH1, LSM11, RNU7-1_ . Patients are also recognized with _AGS-like phenotypes_ in the absence of a known pathogenic variant, suggesting further genetic heterogeneity. The implicated genes encode proteins that are linked in some way to production of IFNs, either due to perturbed nucleic acid metabolism or constitutive activation of nucleic acid sensors. Many of the variants in causative genes are recessively inherited, but autosomal dominant forms are also seen and so a three-generation pedigree is a valuable part of the clinical history (Table 58.1). Classical AGS is a neonatal onset disorder which mimics congenital infection, in the absence of a recognized pathogen. Typical features are fever, hepatosplenomegaly, accompanied by neurological manifestations including irritability, intracranial calcification, and leukodystrophy (Fig. 58.2). Affected children often manifest severe neurological sequalae and may be unable to speak or have purposeful movement. Key investigation findings include elevated inflammatory markers, liver enzyme abnormalities and raised CSF neopterins. In other cases, presentation may be delayed and may occur within or after the first year of life, after an apparently normal period of development, with a sudden loss of skills \n\n**(neuroregression)** . Around 40% of cases also manifest acral chilblains—typically affecting fingers, toes, and sometimes the nose and ear helices (Fig. 58.2). There is also a recognized increased risk of autoimmune disease, hypothyroidism, and glaucoma. In the case of _SAMHD1_ variant, evidence of CNS vasculopathy with **Moyamoya** and/or cerebral aneurysms may be present, either with or without other features of AGS; individuals with _SAMHD1_ variant are at risk of cerebrovascular events. In the case of _ADAR1_ , _IFIH1_ , and _RNASH2B_ variants, **spastic paraparesis** may be the only disease manifestation. _ADAR1_ variant is also causal of bilateral striatal necrosis, which has varying neurological symptoms and distinctive neuroradiological findings with symmetrical degeneration of the putamen, caudate nucleus, and sometimes globus pallidus. Consideration of testing for these genotypes in cases of apparently isolated hereditary spastic paraparesis or bilateral striatal necrosis is therefore warranted. \n\n### **Familial Chilblain Lupus (FCL)** \n\nIsolated FCL without additional features of AGS has been described in some families, particularly in association with _TREX1_ and _SAMHD1_ variants. FCL has also rarely been reported in patients with dominant variants in _STING1_ (previously _TMEM173_ ), more typically associated with **STING-associated vasculopathy of infancy (SAVI)** . However, because of variable expression and clinical penetrance, caution should be exercised when counselling the family, as subsequent offspring may be at risk of more severe syndromes. \n\n### **Singleton-Merten Syndrome (SMS)** \n\nSMS is caused by autosomal dominant variants in either _IFH1_ or _RIGI_ (formerly _DDX58_ ), encoding RNA sensors MDA5 and RIG-I, respectively. The classic features of SMS include glaucoma, psoriasis, aortic calcification, dental dysplasia and skeletal abnormalities including tendon rupture and arthropathy. Typically, these are associated with elevated ISG transcripts. Additionally, in the case of _IFIH1_ associated disease, families with the same variant may variably manifest features of SMS, AGS, and FCL. \n\n**Treatment** for the disorders described above is generally oriented to the particular phenotype, focusing on management of vasculopathy and neurological disability and monitoring for end-organ disease (e.g., cardiovascular disease progression in SMS), allied to attempts in all cases to control autoinflammation with JAK inhibitors (JAKi). \n\n### **Spondyloenchondrodysplasia (SPENCD)** \n\nSecondary to autosomal recessive variants in the acid phosphatase 5, tartrate resistant _(ACP5)_ gene, this condition was originally thought to be a skeletal dysplasia, with enchondromas and platyspondyly, causing progressive short stature. In addition, neurological manifestations (intracranial calcification and white matter disease) accompanied by\n\n<!-- p.1310 -->\n\nintellectual disability, motor defects and seizures may occur. Autoimmune manifestations are frequently reported, with pre-existing diagnoses of childhood-onset SLE and autoimmune thrombocytopenia not infrequent; some children fulfil criteria for multiple autoimmune diseases. Features suggestive of immunodeficiency such as susceptibility to infection are also recognized in some cases, not necessarily accounted for by concomitant immunosuppressive therapy for autoimmune disease. The mechanism(s) linking _ACP5_ variants to ISG upregulation remain obscure. \n\n### **Monogenic Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)** \n\nChildren with typical features of AGS or SPENCD may fulfill the diagnostic criteria for SLE; heterozygous variants in _TREX1, IFIH1,_ or _ACP5_ may be associated with adult onset SLE, without the other associated features. Recognition of such disorders is important because there may be a risk of up to 50% to other family members; targeted therapies such as JAK inhibitors (JAKi) could be considered. Other genes have also been associated with familial monogenic lupus and elevated ISG transcripts and/or IFN-I protein. This includes heterozygous loss of function variants in _DNASE1_ , biallelic _DNASE1L3_ pathogenic variants, biallelic loss of function pathogenic variants in complement 1 (C1q), and gain-offunction variants in _TLR7_ . Of particular note these monogenic forms of lupus have high penetrance; they often have onset in childhood with an equal male to female ratio (with the exception of variants in _TLR7_ ). The typical clinical features in monogenic SLE are renal disease, photosensitive rash, and autoantibodies such as anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm. Variants of genes encoding other complement proteins (C1r/ C1s, C2, C3, C4) have been associated with an increased risk of SLE and autoimmune disease but are not usually considered monogenic forms of SLE because the penetrance is generally lower (see Chapter 60, Complement Disorders). \n\n### **STING-Associated Vasculopathy of Infancy (SAVI) and COPA Syndrome** \n\nGain-of-function variants in _STING1_ (formerly _TMEM173_ ) lead to a severe early-onset vasculopathy, STING-associated vasculopathy of infancy (SAVI). Typically inherited in a dominant pattern, rarer recessive forms have also been described. Predominant features are skin and pulmonary disease, including chilblains, livedo reticularis, ulceration, necrosis and in some cases to autoamputation of digits or loss of tissue (e.g., the nasal septum) (Fig. 58.4). Pulmonary disease, including interstitial lung disease, fibrosis, and hemorrhage, is an important and distinctive feature of this disorder, and can result in progressive respiratory failure necessitating in some cases lung transplantation. Arthritis/ arthropathy is also a well-recognized feature. Additional manifestations include fever, cytopenias, and adenopathy. Investigations typically reveal elevated inflammatory markers, raised ISG transcripts and autoantibodies. A similar \n\nclinical phenotype to SAVI has been reported in **COPA syndrome** , due to heterozygous dominant negative variants in _COPA_ , albeit clinical nonpenetrance has been estimated in approximately 30% of variant carriers. In contrast to many other interferonopathies, the central nervous system does not appear to be affected in either SAVI or COPA syndrome, although intracranial calcification is occasionally seen. SAVI responds to JAK inhibition. \n\n### **_USP18_ and** **_ISG15_ Deficiency,** **_STAT2_ - Associated Type I Interferonopathy** \n\nDisorders of negative feedback upon IFNAR are associated with autosomal recessive variants in _USP18_ and _STAT2,_ exhibiting features of severe, early-onset AGS—also described as **pseudo-TORCH syndrome** . These features include intracranial calcification, leukodystrophy, seizures, neurodevelopmental defects including microcephaly, ventricular dilation and polymicrogyria, fever and thrombocytopenia; these phenotypes are associated with high mortality. Additional manifestations, such as brainstem involvement, intracranial hemorrhage, renal vasculopathy, and pulmonary involvement are less commonly reported in AGS. Survival to adulthood, albeit with significant neurodevelopmental sequelae, has been reported in a case of _STAT2_ -associated type I interferonopathy. Patients with autosomal recessive _ISG15_ deficiency are generally less severely affected, experiencing seizures that are associated with asymptomatic intracranial calcification, consistent with a hypomorphic defect of USP18 protein; necrotizing cutaneous inflammatory disease has also been reported. _ISG15_ deficiency is also associated with the immunodeficiency phenotype **Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease (MSMD)** , in which patients exhibit susceptibility to weakly pathogenic nontuberculous mycobacteria, which is thought to involve a separate function of secreted ISG15 in mycobacterial immunity. Another contributing factor may be suppression of myeloid cell production of IL12 and IL23 by IFN-I, reported in autosomal recessive partial _USP18_ deficiency accompanied by susceptibility to mycobacterial disease. Functional validation of such molecular defects is aided by immunological assays utilizing immunoblotting or flow cytometry methods that demonstrate unusually prolonged JAK-STAT phosphorylation in variant-bearing cells stimulated with IFN-I. Treatment responses to JAK inhibition have been variable, with evidence of suppression of the ISG signature without obvious impact on neuropathology although dosage may be a factor, as control of disease with high-dose ruxolitinib has been reported in a case of _USP18_ deficiency. \n\n### **_SOCS1_ Haploinsufficiency** \n\nHeterozygous variants of _SOCS1_ are associated with a diverse spectrum of immune phenotypes including early-onset autoimmunity (including SLE), autoimmune cytopenia associated with infection and immunodysregulation with\n\n<!-- p.1311 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p1312_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 58.4** Clinical manifestations of stimulator of interferon genes (STING)–associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy (SAVI). (A) Typical facial distribution of telangiectatic lesions on the nose and cheeks with atrophy and scarring of the skin with loss of deep tissue of the nose. (B) Violaceous, scaling, atrophic plaques on both hands and progressive autoamputations of several fingers. (C) Hilar lymphadenopathy and bilateral interstitial infiltrates on high-resolution computed tomography (CT) image. (D) Ulcerated lesions on the pinna of the ear with scales and crusts.(From Sabbagh SE, Verbsky JW. Interferonopathies. In: _Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics_ . 22nd ed. Vol 1. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2025 [Fig. 205.3, p. 1534].) \n\n**common variable immunodeficiency (CVID)** . CNS features more typical of _USP18/STAT2_ lesions or AGS (e.g., calcification, leukodystrophy, neurodevelopmental defects) are absent. While an ISG signature has been reported in _SOCS1_ haploinsufficiency, this is accompanied by a broader pattern of dysregulated cytokine signaling in lymphocytes, potentially accounting for the phenotypic variation. \n\n### **_JAK1_ and** **_STAT1_ Gain-of-Function (GOF)** \n\nClinical features associated with heterozygous GOF variants of _JAK1_ or _STAT1_ are associated with exaggerated ligand-dependent and -independent activation resulting in manifestations which are broader than other interferonopathies. Susceptibility to chronic fungal infection **(chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis)** and organ-specific autoimmunity in _STAT1_ GOF and eosinophilia with immune dysregulation **(hepatosplenomegaly, atopic dermatitis, eosinophilic enteritis)** in _JAK1_ GOF. Other clinical features considered more typical of type I interferonopathy such as intracerebral calcification are less frequently \n\nobserved; neurological phenotypes are generally absent. The broader immune phenotype is presumably explained by the participation of JAK1 and STAT1 in multiple cytokine signaling pathways. JAK inhibition has been reported to be highly effective in case reports of _JAK1_ GOF and _STAT1_ GOF case series. \n\n### **Proteosome-Associated Autoinflammatory Syndrome (PRAAS)** \n\nMonogenic defects of components of the cellular proteosome are associated with autoinflammatory disease phenotypes described by various terms, including chronic atypical neutrophilic dermatosis with lipodystrophy and elevated temperatures **(CANDLE)** , and are known collectively as proteosome-associated autoinflammatory syndromes **(PRAAS)** . The cellular 26S proteosome is a ubiquitously expressed multiprotein complex responsible for catalytic degradation of ubiquitinylated proteins to maintain cellular homeostasis. Loss-of-function variants have been described in genes encoding protein subunits of the constitutive\n\n<!-- p.1312 -->\n\nproteosome (PSMB4 and PSMA3), the immunoproteasome (PSMB8, PSMB9, and PSMB10), as well as proteosome assembly factors (POMP and PSMG2) and the 19S regulatory particle (PSMD12). In most cases these are inherited in a recessive fashion, although _POMP_ and _PSMD12_ variants can be pathogenic in heterozygosity. In some cases, additive loss of function variants in more than one subunit have been described suggestive of digenic inheritance (e.g., _PSMA3/PSMB8_ ). The molecular consequence of these lesions is an accumulation of cytosolic ubiquitinylated proteins triggering the unfolded protein response (UPR), although precise mechanistic links to ISG induction at the molecular level remain uncertain. \n\nClinical phenotypes associated with PRAAS generally differ from classical interferonopathies such as AGS, although common features such as intracranial calcification are recognized. Distinctive features of PRAAS include lipoatrophy/panniculitis, chronic neutrophilic dermatosis, muscle atrophy, and joint contractures, accompanied by cytopenia and immunological defects (dysgammaglobulinemia). Treatment may include glucocorticoids (especially in disease flares) and more targeted therapies such as JAK inhibitors. \n\nMonogenic defects of the 19S regulatory particle are associated with a discrete neurodevelopmental disease **(Stankiewicz-Isidor syndrome)** without overt inflammatory disease, which may relate to tissue-restricted expression of the 19S regulatory particle, although subclinical ISG signatures have been reported. \n\nbeen linked to reductions in cytosolic RNA:DNA hybrids, reminiscent of variants in the _RNASEH2_ complex. \n\n### **_ATAD3A_ Deficiency** \n\nHeterozygous dominant-negative variants of the gene encoding ATPase family AAA domain–containing protein 3A (ATAD3A), a mitochondrial membrane protein are linked to IFN-I system activation (elevated IFNα protein and positive ISG transcriptional signatures) in several patients exhibiting variable neurological and autoimmune phenotypes (including systemic sclerosis). There was marked variability in expression in related family members. At the cellular level, knockdown of _ATAD3A_ expression led to induction of IFN-I signaling, which could be attenuated by mtDNA depletion. The link to interferonopathy appears to derive from the capacity of mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) to engage cGAS-STING signaling. \n\n### **_PNPTI_ Deficiency** \n\nPatients bearing compound heterozygous/homozygous hypomorphic variants in _PNPT1_ , which encodes the enzyme polynucleotide phosphorylase (PNPase), exhibit cellular mitochondrial double-stranded RNA accumulation accompanied by MDA5-dependent upregulation of ISGs. _PNPT1_ deficiency was previously identified as a cause of infantile mitochondrial disease with associated neuroimaging features including bilateral striatal necrosis, suggestive of overlap between interferonopathy and mitochondrionopathy. \n\n### **_DNASE2_ Deficiency** \n\nBiallelic loss of function variants in _DNASE2_ , encoding the lysosomal exonuclease DNase II, is associated with a systemic autoinflammatory disease characterized variously by cytopenias, deforming arthropathy, membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, liver fibrosis and elevated anti-DNA autoantibodies, accompanied by elevated IFNα protein and ISG transcript expression in peripheral blood. Neurological disease was not observed, although subtle subcortical white matter changes were apparent on neuroimaging. DNase II is responsible for degrading lysosomal DNA in myeloid cells. Interestingly, _Dnase2a_ knockout in mice leads to diverse pathology, including lethal IFN-I dependent anemia and deforming arthropathy in _Dnase2a_ −/− _Ifnar1_ −/− mice, illustrating the potential of disease phenotypes to be both IFN-dependent and IFN-independent, with obvious implications for treatment selection. \n\n### **_POLA1_ Deficiency** \n\nAnother putative type I interferonopathy due to an intronic _POLA1_ variant, also known as X-linked reticulate pigmentary disorder, exhibits immunodeficiency features, autoinflammation and hyperpigmentation, is associated with a peripheral blood ISG transcriptional signature but without other clinical phenotypes more typical of AGS. Mechanistically, this has \n\n## **Approaches to Clinical Investigation** \n\n### **IFN-I Biomarkers** \n\nBiomarkers of IFN-I activity play an important role in the clinical assessment of patients with suspected type I interferonopathy phenotypes and serve as an indication of the need for further genetic analysis (see below). Unfortunately, these assays are not yet part of standard medical laboratory testing, generally necessitating collaboration with academic research laboratories. An important consideration is the very high bioactivity of IFN-I molecules such as IFNα subtypes, which are active at extremely low concentration (fg/ mL). Traditional methods of direct detection of IFN-I proteins, such as enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA), are insufficiently sensitive and assays previously relied on measuring functional aspects of downstream IFN-I signaling. Approaches to measure ISG transcription in patient samples by reverse-transcriptase PCR and using higherthroughput methods such as Nanostring has facilitated the identification of a range of new and previously unrecognized type I interferonopathies. This is sometimes referred to in the literature as an _ISG signature_ . ISG transcription is induced by all IFN subtypes; therefore, the ISG signature is not necessarily a specific readout of IFN-I activity. Developments in digital ELISA/single molecule array (SiMoA)\n\n<!-- p.1313 -->\n\ntechnology (using high-affinity autoantibodies from autoimmune regulator _(AIRE)_ deficient patients) have enabled the detection of IFNα subtypes in patient samples with vastly greater sensitivity. Practical constraints mean that certain assays are not applicable to all clinical sample types. ISG transcript assays are generally not performed on CSF due to the paucity of cellular material, while samples for digital ELISA must be stored at −80°C and transported on dry ice. Given the potential of intercurrent infection to induce a false positive result in a single sample, serial testing is typically employed, ideally when patients are clinically free of infection. IFN-I biomarkers do not necessarily correlate with clinical expression of disease phenotype. Patients bearing a type I interferonopathy-associated variant in _IFIH1_ may exhibit a persistent ISG signature for many years without development of overt disease. Conversely, a significant minority (~27%) of patients with AGS associated with _RNASEHB2_ variants lack a peripheral blood ISG signature. Biomarker data should be taken in context as part of a multifaceted clinical assessment. \n\n### **Genetic Testing and Analysis** \n\nThere is marked phenotypic overlap between type I interferonopathies, reinforcing the need for genetic analysis to ascertain the underlying genotype. Taking AGS as an example, there are few clinical cues, such as Moya Moya with _SAMHD1_ variants, as to which of the several causative genotypes might underlie disease, meaning that molecular diagnosis ultimately relies on genetic testing. The priority is to identify a constellation of clinical features or syndromes that should prompt further assessment of biomarker activity and ultimately genetic testing (see Table 58.1). Whole exome or genome sequencing can assay all known causative genes simultaneously or alternatively focus analysis (and reporting) on a panel of genes considered most pertinent to the clinical phenotype. In either scenario, comprehensive clinical data, family history, and pertinent investigations such as biomarkers of IFN-I activity and imaging results is of paramount importance in the interpretation of the genetic results. Such detailed phenotyping data is also crucial in cases where a VUS is identified. \n\n## **Principles of Management** \n\n### **Clinical Management** \n\nMultidisciplinary supportive measures are central to patient management, including nutritional support, physiotherapy and occupational therapy, with specific considerations for clinical monitoring depending on underlying genotype (Table 58.3). There remain relatively few disease-modifying therapeutic options for type I interferonopathies. Because of the association with excessive IFN-I activity, therapeutic blockade of IFN-I signaling, primarily with JAK inhibitors (JAKi, mainly ruxolitinib, baricitinib and tofacitinib), is a cornerstone of treatment. Open label studies indicate benefit \n\n#### **TABLE Disease-Specific Management 58.3 Considerations** \n\nMultidisciplinary management is often required owing to the multisystem nature of these disorders; however, some specific considerations are outlined below. \n\n|AGS|Screening for glaucoma and<br>hypothyroidism, monitoring of liver<br>function, provision of antiepileptics,<br>enteral nutritional support|\n|---|---|\n|_SAMHD1_<br>variant|Magnetic resonance angiography (MRA)<br>at diagnosis, neurovascular referral<br>given risk of stroke and intracerebral<br>hemorrhage|\n|SAVI/COPA<br>syndrome|Pulmonary function monitoring, respiratory<br>referral—lung transplantation has been<br>required|\n|SMS|Cardiac imaging and cardiology<br>referral (aortic disease is a recognized<br>complication)|\n|_AGS,_<br>Aicardi-Go<br>syndrome;_SAVI,_S<br>_SMS,_Singleton-M|utières<br>syndrome;<br>_COPA,_<br>coatomer<br>subunit-α<br>TING-associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy;<br>erten syndrome.|\n\n\nin terms of control of systemic inflammation and cutaneous disease manifestations. One question concerns the impact of JAKi on neurological disease phenotypes, perhaps relating to issues about CNS penetrance and/or timing of treatment initiation in relation to the onset of potentially irreversible neurological damage. Given the natural history of AGS, it is conceivable that treatment initiation _after_ CNS disease is established may come too late to provide benefit, implying that disease modifying therapy might be necessary prior to the onset of clinical disease. Undoubtedly there are questions about how to deploy JAKi most effectively in the type I interferonopathies. A case report of effective control of _USP18_ deficiency with high dose ruxolitinib suggests that a higher dose may be required. Another therapeutic strategy under evaluation in clinical trials is the repurposed use of the nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) zidovudine, lamivudine and abacavir with the rationale of inhibiting the accumulation of immunostimulatory DNA. The development of more specific modes of IFN-I blockade, such as a monoclonal antibody targeting IFNAR1, anifrolumab, might offer an additional treatment avenue that awaits formal clinical assessment. \n\n### **Genetic Counseling** \n\nDifferent modes of inheritance are recognized for the type I interferonopathies, mainly autosomal recessive or dominant and in the case of _POLA1_ and _TLR7_ defects, X-linked. In a minority of genotypes, e.g., _TREX1_ and _STING1_ , both recessive and dominant modes of inheritance are recognized. Understanding the pattern of inheritance is necessary to inform discussion of transmission risk and clinical implications\n\n<!-- p.1314 -->\n\nfor family members. If counselling is required to explain risk and to consider family testing, referral to a geneticist should be considered, particularly given the fact that clinical penetrance and expression can vary markedly. Genetic counselling can also inform family planning choices and preimplantation genetic diagnosis. \n"});
